Experiment: GitHub Webhook Integration with Jenkins

1. Create a GitHub repository named "git4".

2. Create "file1.txt" and push it to GitHub.

3. Open Jenkins and create a Freestyle Project named "git4".

4. Under Source Code Management, select Git and enter the repository URL: "https://github.com/kavya080206-stack/git4.git"

5. Set Branch Specifier to "*/main".

6. Under Build Triggers, select GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling.

7. Under Build Steps, select Execute Windows batch command and enter:

   echo github webhook triggered
   git --version

8. Click Save and select Build Now.

9. Open the build number and click Console Output. Verify "Finished: SUCCESS".

10. Download and extract ngrok from "https://ngrok.com/download".

11. Open Command Prompt inside the folder containing "ngrok.exe".

12. Sign up or log in at "https://dashboard.ngrok.com" and copy your authtoken from "https://dashboard.ngrok.com/get-started/your-authtoken".

13. Run the following command, replacing YOUR_AUTHTOKEN with your actual token:

    ngrok config add-authtoken YOUR_AUTHTOKEN

14. Start ngrok:

    ngrok http 8080

15. Copy the HTTPS Forwarding URL displayed by ngrok. Keep ngrok running.

16. In GitHub, go to Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook.

17. Enter the Payload URL as "https://YOUR-NGROK-URL/github-webhook/" (replace with your active ngrok URL).

18. Set Content type to "application/json".

19. Select the "Just the push event" option and click Add webhook.

20. Open Git Bash in the local "git4" folder and execute:

    echo "Testing webhook" >> file1.txt
    git add file1.txt
    git commit -m "Test GitHub webhook"
    git push origin main

21. Check Jenkins Build History. A new build should start automatically.

22. Open Console Output and verify "Finished: SUCCESS".
